# The build context is the repo root, so without this every `docker build` ships the
# host's node_modules, .venv and .git to the daemon — several hundred megabytes — and
# Dockerfile.caddy's `COPY web/ ./` drops the host's resolved node_modules on top of
# the tree pnpm just installed inside the image. On a machine whose host platform
# differs from the image's, that alone breaks the build on esbuild/rollup binaries.
.git
.venv
**/node_modules
**/__pycache__
**/*.pyc
web/dist
web/dev-dist
web/test-results
web/playwright-report
*.bundle

# Every env-file variant, anywhere. A developer's .env.local or .env.production.local
# would otherwise reach the daemon and be baked into the build stage's cache by
# `COPY web/ ./`. Nothing in an image build needs one.
**/.env
**/.env.*
**/*.local
